  "projects": [
   {
    "title": "Lord of the Rings Living Card Game",
    "description": "Lord of the Rings Living Card Game is a cooperative deck building game originally created by Fantasy Flight in which players construct their own deck to overcome a pre-constructed quest deck consisting of quest cards, and a deck of threats call the encounter deck.  This card game has a wide variety of abilities and card types which include, but are note limited to: healing, equipment, attacking, defending, card drawing, etc. Currently in my project it only supports one player, the decks are pre-constructed, and only a few abilities have been implemented into the game; however, the direction of this project is to eventually integrate the ability of supporting multiple players, allowing players to create their own decks, and be able to support all of the unique card effects in this game.  I am presently updating the project weekly to bi-weekly. \n\nThe tools that I am using to create this project are JAVA Swing and other JAVA standard libraries, as well as utilizing this opportunity to use object oriented programming with an emphasis on inheritance. \n\nI originally began this project because it was one of my favorite card games, I knew the rules quite well, and I wanted the opportunity to program a large scale project that gave me the chance to practice creating maintainable code as well as allowing me to deal with multiple different exceptions in the rules of the game. \n\nA link to the rules is supplied here: \nhttps://images-cdn.fantasyflightgames.com/filer_public/d0/de/d0de4155-2d2a-40dd-820d-f19f7bd75700/lotr_lcg_core_rules_eng_lo-res.pdf",
    "dateRange": {},
    "associatedWith": "Nov 2017 - Present"
   },
   {
    "title": "Various other small projects",
    "description": "This is a collection of various other past projects I created to improve my skills as a programmer.  Here is what is currently included: \n\nSudoku (JAVA): Allowed me to first start using and implementing JAVA Swing. \n\nFreeCell (JAVA): A game that required multiple classes and used basic object oriented programming.",
    "dateRange": {},
    "associatedWith": "Jan 2017 - Present"
   }
